{"duration_api_ms":42541,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"d3dda01b-57e9-4016-a25b-7e0a00945da0","total_cost_usd":0.1976158,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11032,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":5453,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2040},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11032,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5453,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11032,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11032},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":5453,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11032,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1976158,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":2040,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# BIP: 39\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThis BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\\n\\nIt consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed. This seed can be later used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\\n\\n## Copyright\\n\\nThis BIP is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license.\\n\\n## Motivation\\n\\nA mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed. The sentence could be written on paper or spoken over the telephone.\\n\\nThis guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription. It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\\n\\n## Generating the mnemonic\\n\\nThe mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits. With more entropy, security is improved but the sentence length increases. The initial entropy length is referred to as ENT. The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\\n\\nFirst, an initial entropy of ENT bits is generated. A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash. This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy. Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist. Finally, these numbers are converted into words and the joined words are used as the mnemonic sentence.\\n\\nThe following table describes the relation between the initial entropy length (ENT), the checksum length (CS), and the length of the generated mnemonic sentence (MS) in words.\\n\\n```\\nCS = ENT / 32\\nMS = (ENT + CS) / 11\\n\\n|  ENT  | CS | ENT+CS |  MS  |\\n+-------+----+--------+------+\\n|  128  |  4 |   132  |  12  |\\n|  160  |  5 |   165  |  15  |\\n|  192  |  6 |   198  |  18  |\\n|  224  |  7 |   231  |  21  |\\n|  256  |  8 |   264  |  24  |\\n```\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0039.mediawiki\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"heading and position of the mnemonic generation section\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"## Generating the memonic\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"## Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"Misspelling fixed; placed after Motivation, following the base's order\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 mnemonics are groups of words that generate deterministic wallets\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 converts the mnemonic into a binary seed, not an ASCII one\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license.\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license, not the GPL\"},{\"segment\":\"a9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"The raw representation handled is the wallet seed, not satoshis\"},{\"segment\":\"a11\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"reason\":\"The mnemonic transports computer-generated randomness, not determinism\"},{\"segment\":\"a12\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"süeed is a misspelling of seed\"},{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"## Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"memonic is a misspelling of mnemonic\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"reason\":\"demonic is a typo; entropy is a multiple of 32 bits, per CS = ENT / 32\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"With more entropy, security is improved but the sentence length increases.\",\"reason\":\"Punctuation only\"},{\"segment\":\"b4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The initial entropy length is referred to as ENT.\",\"reason\":\"Recast impersonally\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"reason\":\"ENT is 128-256 bits, as the table itself shows\"},{\"segment\":\"b7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"reason\":\"The hash is SHA256; SHA652 does not exist\"},{\"segment\":\"b8\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 appends the checksum to the end of the entropy\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"reason\":\"Groups are 11 bits (0-2047), matching MS = (ENT + CS) / 11\"},{\"segment\":\"b10\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Finally, these numbers are converted into words and the joined words are used as the mnemonic sentence.\",\"reason\":\"Recast impersonally\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"corrects\":\"a group of easy to remember letters -- for the generation of deterministic UTOXs\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Abstract (bitcoin/bips repository)\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 mnemonics are word sequences used to derive deterministic wallets\"},{\"statement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"corrects\":\"converting it into a ASCII seed\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Abstract\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 derives a 512-bit binary seed via PBKDF2\"},{\"statement\":\"This BIP is licensed under the 2-clause BSD license.\",\"corrects\":\"This BIP falls under the GPL License.\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Copyright section\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39's Copyright section names the 2-clause BSD license\"},{\"statement\":\"A mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed.\",\"corrects\":\"representations of a wallet satoshis\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Motivation\",\"reason\":\"The Motivation compares mnemonics with raw representations of a wallet seed\"},{\"statement\":\"This guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated randomness with a human-readable transcription.\",\"corrects\":\"computer-generated determinism\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Motivation\",\"reason\":\"The mnemonic encodes random entropy, not determinism\"},{\"statement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"a multiple of 23 bits\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"CS = ENT / 32 in the document's own table requires multiples of 32\"},{\"statement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"corrects\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 1024-2048 byts.\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"The document's own table runs ENT from 128 to 256 bits\"},{\"statement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"corrects\":\"SHA652 hash\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 uses SHA256; no SHA652 algorithm exists\"},{\"statement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"corrects\":\"appended to the start of the initial entropy\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"BIP 39 appends the checksum after the entropy bits\"},{\"statement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"corrects\":\"split into groups of 12 bits\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP 39, Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Generating the mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"0-2047 is 11 bits, and the table has MS = (ENT + CS) / 11\"}]}","ttft_ms":19768,"type":"result","duration_ms":42608,"uuid":"ba426894-363b-40a0-99e6-1aebb5388854","ttft_stream_ms":1238,"time_to_request_ms":67,"first_content_frame_ms":1239,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
